A||||12||William||Black trim plates. Could it be this simple?||||1||
Z||000000||William||12-16-2000||03:06 AM||||After deciding to go with a tan / black ferarri style interior color, (already started!),I now feel that wood veneer trim plates would look wrong with it. I think I need to go with black trim plates, but I'm not sure how to do it. I had thought about custom cut black formica caps or so type of shiny black vinyl stick on sheets. I think the kit car guys just paint their aluminum plates, (like on the pisa dash). Why wouldn't this work for me? Maybe a black base coat then a clear coat with that plasticote stuff that worked so good on my rims. I could sand with 2000 grit and buff if needed. Would it look painted,(homemade), or like factory plastic? I know some here would like black plates, but I don't know anyone here that has done it and that concerns me somewhat. There has to be a reason :-) It would save about 300.00 if I didn't need the wood stuff and I could always go that route if it didn't come out very good. My current panels are scratched up anyway...  Thoughts?||||reg||1
Z||000001||DaRkLoRD||12-16-2000||03:31 AM||||I've toyed with the idea of using painted plexiglass.... paint the side that will be attached to the dash.. that way it'll look glossy.<P>make sense?<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||3
Z||000002||tgowens||12-16-2000||03:31 AM||||we have a customer who painted the trim fascias on his '88 Formula with a black wrinkle finish paint and it really looks good - you might consider something along that line - just a thought......||||reg||1
Z||000003||deceler8||12-16-2000||12:43 PM||||My car had the silver finish trim plates, with some of the silver rubbed down to the black plastic.<P>After looking around for nicer ones (and suffering the sticker shock), I sanded all the silver off mine, wet sanded them, and polished the plastic with my Dremel. They have a nice black gloss finish and look great.<P>A cheap way out. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||RNGCRSH||12-16-2000||12:54 PM||||The plexiglass Idea is good. instad of plexiglass use Lexan. the reason for lexan is there is a special paint,sold at hobby stores, that is used for RC cars that bonds to lexan and will not crack from the heat or minor scratches etc. You paint the back side with this paint also.||||reg||1
Z||000005||William||12-16-2000||01:15 PM||||Deler8,<BR>Do you have any pics? I'd love to see them! I think I might try your method this weekend. It's it doesn't look right. I could try to clear coat the raw plastic. It that goes bad I can still resand and do the base / clear coat method with just recovering them with lexan or formica as the last option. I don't see what I have to lose here.||||reg||1
Z||000006||jhar1987||12-16-2000||11:55 PM||||i painted mine with white basecoat clearcoat out of my hvlp gun. they look great. if i get a chance i'll take some pics.||||reg||1
Z||000007||rogergarrison||12-17-2000||07:23 AM||||On my Ferrari kit, i made new face for main gauges and accessory (oil and amp) out of lexan. i made the cutouts for oil,amp,gas, and fuel round instead of rectangles. Ferrari are always round. I painted the main face silver and ass black because thats the way they are on original - aluminum. ive got pics if youd like to see<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=9116379&p=34556367">  <p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 12-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||rogergarrison||12-17-2000||07:31 AM||||heres page <A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumIndex?u1234884&a=9116479" TARGET=_blank>http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumIndex?u=1234884&a=9116479</A> <p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 12-17-2000).]||||reg||1
